this isn't a zombie show and I know  there's a zombie on screen but the last  of us is really a show about humans  even so it'll probably make the zombie  genre explode all over again and give  new life to the corpse that is zombie TV  over the past several years we've been  inundated with zombie TV shows and  movies that all look and feel kind of  the same and now we have this so you  might be wondering how exactly is The  Last of Us not a zombie show and to  answer that we'll have to discuss a few  things about the series character  Humanity World building and  oversaturation also we're going to talk  about how the series will revive the  zombie genre as a whole and give us a  new take on where the genre should be  headed first things first though we have  to talk about the show's character for  those unfamiliar with the PlayStation  game the series is based on the Last of  Us follows the story of Joel and Ellie  as they navigate a post-apocalyptic  World filled with deadly infected  creatures but this isn't just another  zombie story The Infected in the last of  us are not reanimated corpses but rather  humans who have been infected with the  fungus that's taken over their bodies  and turned them into monstrous creatures  this this small change to the  traditional zombie formula adds an  interesting twist and helps set the last  of us apart from other stories in the  genre but it's not just the infected  that sets The Last of Us Apart what  really makes this a standout story is  its emotional depth and complex  characters Joel and Ellie's relationship  is the heart of the story and their  character development is what truly  drives the narrative forward Joel is a  grizzled Survivor who's been through  hell and back and is struggling to come  to terms with the loss of his loved ones  Ellie on the other hand is a young girl  who's never known a world without the  infected and is trying to find her place  in this new dangerous World watching  these two characters grow and change as  they journey together is what makes the  last of us such a special and memorable  experience it's this emotional core  that'll make the HBO adaptation stand  out in the crowded World of Zombie media  the show has the potential to bring a  fresh perspective to the genre and  appeal to large audiences Beyond just  fans of the game too often zombie  stories are all about the action in the  gore and while those elements can  certainly be enjoyable they can also get  old pretty quickly The Last of Us on the  other hand is just as much about the  characters and their relationships as it  is about the infected and the action  this focus on character development and  emotion will bring a much needed breadth  of fresh air to the zombie genre The  Last of Us also tackle some pretty heavy  themes such as loss trauma and the cost  of survival in a way that few zombie  stories have before the game deals with  these themes in a mature and nuanced way  and the show has the potential to  continue this trend and delve even  deeper into these themes in a world  filled with Undead creatures in constant  danger it's only natural that characters  would be affected by trauma and loss the  show has the opportunity to explore  these themes in a way that feels  authentic and meaningful rather than  just using them as shallow plot points  but this brings up an important theme  that's Central to the argument that The  Last of Us isn't a zombie story and it's  that Humanity not zombies is the real  problem if you're familiar with the game  then you know that this is a theme that  runs throughout the story The Infected  are certainly a threat but it's the  other humans that the characters  encounter who are often the most  dangerous from Ruthless Bandits and  scavengers to well-meaning survivors who  will do whatever it takes to protect  their own the characters of the last of  us are constantly faced with the fact  that humans are just as dangerous as the  infected this theme is particularly  relevant in today's world where it  sometimes feels like humanity is Its Own  Worst Enemy from political strife and  social division to environmental  destruction it's easy to feel like the  real monsters are the humans around us  and The Last of Us touches on all of  these issues in a way that feels  relevant and timely in a world where  they infected are a constant threat it's  easy to see them as the enemy but the  last of us reminds us that it's often  Humanity that's the real danger it's  this theme that makes the last of us  such a relevant and thought-provoking  story in addition to its Incredible  World building the intricate and  well-developed environments in the game  are top-notch and the show has the  opportunity to expand on them even  further the showrunners Craig Mason and  Neil druckman have the freedom to  explore new story lines and expand on  the lore of the world beyond anything  we've seen before first off the cast is  absolutely incredible Joel is is being  played by Pedro Pascal who you may know  from his roles in Game of Thrones in the  Mandalorian and Ellie is being played by  Bella Ramsey who absolutely stole the  show as lyanna morma on Game of Thrones  these two actors have incredible  chemistry and it's clear that they're  going to bring Joel and Ellie to life in  a way that fans of the game will love  recently Pascal revealed that he and  Ramsay clicked instantly on set saying  it helped a lot to have Bella to  navigate the world of the last of us we  were stepping into this experience at  the same time and there was an immediate  Bond we've also gotten to see the  incredible attention to detail that the  show's production team is bringing to  the adaptation from the sets and costume  to the makeup and special effects  everything looks absolutely spot-on it's  clear that the team behind the show is  doing everything they can to make sure  that the adaptation stays true to the  spirit of the game and of course we  can't forget about the infected these  monstrous creatures are a key part of  the last of us and it looks like the  show's team is pulling out all the stops  to bring them to life in a way that's  both terrifying and realistic based on  what we've seen so far the infected are  going to be a highlight of the show and  will definitely give viewers some jump  scares lastly though let's talk about  the zombie genres over saturation it's  no secret that the zombie genre has been  over saturated in recent years with  countless movies TV shows and video  games featuring Undead creatures  shuffling about and feasting on human  flesh it's easy to feel like you've seen  it all before and it can be hard for new  entries in the genre to stand out but  that's where the Last of Us comes in  this series has the potential to  completely revive the zombie genre but  how exactly will it do this it's simple  by bringing something new to the table  the emotional depth and complex  characters of The Last of Us will set it  apart from other zombie stories and  bring a fresh perspective to a genre  that's felt washed out since the fifth  season of The Walking Dead The Last of  Us have the opportunity to appeal to  audiences Beyond just fans of the game  and bring in new viewers who may have  grown tired of the typical zombie story  Additionally the show's focus on  character development and World building  will help it stand out in a crowded  field too often zombie stories rely on  on cheap scares and Gore to carry the  narrative but the last of us has the  potential to go beyond that and explore  deeper themes and story lines it also  points the way for where zombie stories  should focus in the future not on the  undead creatures or blood and gore but  on the human conflicts and personal  sticks that arise because of a zombie  outbreak if there's one takeaway the  zombie genre should gain from studying  The Last of Us it's that they should  take a more grounded approach just like  the hardest difficulty in The Last of Us  game it's impossible to predict exactly  how the show will turn out but based on  the strength of the source material  there's no doubt that the last of us has  the potential to be a standout in the  zombie genre it has the opportunity to  bring something new and fresh to the  table and Revitalize a genre that's been  feeling a bit stale so don't be fooled  by the zombie aspect of the show The  Last of Us is so much more than that  it's a story about love loss and the  things we do for the people we care  about so it's not a zombie show it's a  story about the human condition and has  the potential to move and Inspire  audiences in a way that few others shows  can and I can't wait to see what it has  in store for us
 

